<%# locals: (agent_name:, stream:, channel:, messages:, composer:, empty: nil) %>
<%#
  One agent web chat: the live stream, the thread, the typing slot and the composer
  (the shell's lower half: agent_chats/_shell). Any heading agent, any thread: the
  caller names the agent, the signed stream and the channel that admits it, the turns
  (AgentMessage rows, oldest first), and the composer's locals (url, scope, id, error,
  text; agent_chats/_form). The DOM ids are AgentMessages::WebChat's targets (still
  named cto-chat-*), so its broadcasts land here, and ONE PAGE HOLDS ONE CHAT: a
  second on the same page would share the ids. The page around it (portrait, status
  card, notices) is the caller's. app/javascript/agent_chat.js keeps it honest.
%>
<%= turbo_stream_from stream, channel: channel %>
<div class="flex flex-col gap-3" data-test="cto-chat">
  <div id="<%= AgentMessages::WebChat::MESSAGES_TARGET %>" role="log" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Conversation with <%= agent_name %>"
       class="flex flex-col gap-4 px-1 py-2 overflow-y-auto max-h-[60vh]" data-test="cto-chat-messages" data-cto-chat-scroll>
    <% if empty %>
      <p class="hidden only:block text-center text-secondary text-sm py-6" data-test="cto-chat-empty"><%= empty %></p>
    <% end %>
    <% messages.each do |message| %>
      <%= render "agent_chats/turn", message: message, agent_name: agent_name %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <div id="<%= AgentMessages::WebChat::TYPING_TARGET %>" class="px-1 empty:hidden" data-test="cto-chat-typing-slot"
       data-fade-seconds="<%= AgentMessages::WebChat::TYPING_FADE %>"></div>
  <%= render "agent_chats/form", agent_name: agent_name, **composer %>
</div>
